# likeness: the ground-truth schema, entry point.
#
# This file is the whole specification of the application in machine-checkable
# form. Everything downstream - the five ports, the capability refusals, the
# `describe` document, the agent tool descriptions, the docs and the parity
# corpora - is checked against it or generated from it.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
#
# SPEC.LIKENESS.md in project-specs remains the reasoned source of record: it
# says why. This is the testable projection: it says exactly what, in a form a
# machine refuses to let drift. Where the two disagree, that is a bug in one of
# them and it is fixed in the same commit, never left as a difference of
# emphasis.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#
#   aontu vet def/<shape>.aon <data>.aon    check one data file against its shape
#   make spec-check                          check every data file in this repo
#   aontu jsonschema def/likeness.aon        export for a consumer that wants JSON Schema
#   aontu breaking --against <rev> ...       is this change breaking?
#
# A SCHEMA FILE DOES NOT EVALUATE ON ITS OWN, and that is correct rather than a
# fault: a shape is a truth, not a value, so `aontu def/likeness.aon` reports
# what it has no value for. Use `vet`.
#
# A DATA FILE MUST NOT IMPORT ITS OWN SHAPE. `vet` composes the two. A data file
# that imports its shape puts the shape's declarations on the data side, where
# they read as unsatisfied requirements and the verdict comes back `incomplete`.

@"./base.aon"
@"./note.aon"
@"./source.aon"
@"./capability.aon"
@"./error.aon"
@"./envelope.aon"
@"./command.aon"
@"./corpus.aon"
@"./project.aon"
@"./replica.aon"
